204 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 131 0 R/E(The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime \(UNODC\) operates under the broader UN system and works through international cooperation, policy development, research, and technical assistance. Its work is organized around key areas including drug control, crime prevention, criminal justice, corruption, terrorism, and transnational organized crime. UNODC collaborates with Member States, international organizations, and other stakeholders to develop legal frameworks, strengthen national institutions, and coordinate global responses to emerging security challenges.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

205 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 131 0 R/T(Structure)/E(Structure)/Pg 13 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

206 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 131 0 R/E(UNODC supports Member States in addressing illicit drugs, transnational organized crime, corruption and terrorism-related issues through research, normative support, technical assistance and capacity-building. Its work links criminal justice, international cooperation and prevention.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

207 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 131 0 R/T(Mandate and Powers)/E(Mandate and Powers)/Pg 13 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

208 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 131 0 R/E(The financing of terrorism and transnational organized crime poses a significant threat to international peace, security, and economic stability. Financial Intelligence Units \(FIUs\) play a crucial role in identifying and disrupting illicit financial flows by collecting, analyzing, and sharing financial intelligence related to suspicious transactions. This agenda focuses on strengthening cooperation between FIUs, improving information-sharing mechanisms, and enhancing national and international frameworks to prevent criminals and terrorist organizations from exploiting financial systems.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

209 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(Introduction to the Agenda)/E(Introduction to the Agenda)/Pg 13 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

210 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 131 0 R/T(WHY financial intelligence is central)/E(WHY financial intelligence is central)/Pg 13 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

211 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 131 0 R/E(Criminal organizations need money to operate, move goods, corrupt officials and conceal proceeds. Terrorist organizations also require resources for recruitment, logistics and operations. Financial investigations can reveal networks that may be difficult to identify through traditional evidence alone.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

