252 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(History & Legal Architecture)/E(History & Legal Architecture)/Pg 15 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

253 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 133 0 R/T(UNTOC \204 the Palermo Convention)/E(UNTOC \204 the Palermo Convention)/Pg 15 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

254 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, adopted in 2000, marked a major step in developing a common international legal framework for combating organized criminal groups and related offences. Its significance for financial intelligence is that organized crime is not only a problem of violence or illicit markets; criminal groups depend on the movement, concealment and integration of proceeds. Financial investigation therefore becomes a way of disrupting the economic infrastructure that sustains criminal activity.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

255 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(UNTOC also illustrates why transnational crime cannot be addressed solely through domestic prosecution. Evidence, suspects, companies, assets and transactions may be distributed across several jurisdictions. Mutual legal assistance, extradition, international cooperation and information-sharing therefore form part of the broader architecture within which FIUs operate.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

256 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime was adopted by General Assembly resolution 55/25 in 2000, opened for signature in Palermo, Italy, and entered into force in 2003. It is the main international instrument addressing transnational organized crime and is supplemented by three protocols on trafficking in persons, migrant smuggling and firearms.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

257 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 133 0 R/T(Terrorist Financing Convention)/E(Terrorist Financing Convention)/Pg 15 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

258 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The International Convention for the Suppression of the Financing of Terrorism, adopted in 1999, was an important development because it directly addressed financing as a distinct component of counter-terrorism. The Convention defines \215funds\216 broadly, including tangible and intangible assets and electronic or digital instruments, and requires States to establish measures for prevention, prosecution and international cooperation.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

259 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The historical significance is that international counter-terrorism policy increasingly moved beyond responding only to terrorist attacks toward disrupting the systems that make attacks possible. Financial intelligence became central to that approach because tracing money can reveal networks, facilitators and logistical relationships that may not be visible through traditional intelligence alone.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

260 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The 1999 International Convention for the Suppression of the Financing of Terrorism requires states to criminalize terrorist financing and provides a framework for identifying, freezing and seizing funds connected to terrorist activity.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

261 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 133 0 R/T(Security Council framework)/E(Security Council framework)/Pg 15 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

262 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(The Security Council subsequently strengthened the international framework through binding measures addressing terrorist financing, targeted sanctions and the financing of terrorism as a threat to international peace and security. These measures interact with, rather than replace, treaty obligations and domestic law. For delegates, this distinction matters because a Council resolution can create or reinforce international obligations, while the detailed mechanics of FIU powers, criminal procedure and financial regulation usually remain matters of national implementation.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

263 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[656 0 R 0]/K[18]>>
endobj

264 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[665 0 R 0]/K[19]>>
endobj

