<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 134 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[662 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

275 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 134 0 R/T(FATF and Egmont)/E(FATF and Egmont)/Pg 16 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

276 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(FATF develops global AML/CFT standards; the Egmont Group facilitates cooperation among FIUs. These are important partners in understanding the operational ecosystem, but they are not UN bodies. Delegates should describe them accurately)/Pg 16 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

277 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(he historical development therefore runs from general international cooperation, to specific terrorist-financing obligations, to increasingly sophisticated financial-sanctions and information-sharing mechanisms. Modern financial intelligence policy sits at the intersection of these layers.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

278 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Security Council resolution 1373 \(2001\) requires states to prevent and suppress terrorist financing. Resolution 2462 \(2019\) specifically strengthens the Council\220s focus on disrupting terrorist financing and improving implementation. The 1267/1989/2253 sanctions regime adds targeted measures against listed ISIL/Al-Qaida-associated individuals and entities.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

279 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 134 0 R/T(Current situation)/E(Current situation)/Pg 16 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

280 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(In July 2025, FATF published its Comprehensive Update on Terrorist Financing Risks, its most extensive global assessment to date, drawing on contributions from more than 80 jurisdictions to map how terrorist actors currently raise, move, store and spend funds. The report found that 69 per cent of jurisdictions assessed showed major or structural deficiencies in effectively investigating, prosecuting and convicting terrorist-financing cases, indicating that formal criminalization has outpaced actual enforcement capacity in much of the world.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

281 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(FATF's ongoing plenary work continues to track how financing methods are evolving. Its June 2026 plenary approved a new publication on detecting and disrupting terrorist financing conducted through social media, instant messaging applications and streaming platforms, building directly on the 2025 comprehensive update, while an October 2025 plenary approved new guidance on asset recovery and an initial horizon-scan on the use of artificial intelligence and deepfakes in illicit finance. The Security Council's own Counter-Terrorism Committee, through CTED, has welcomed this work and connected it to its 2025 non-binding Algeria Guiding Principles on new and emerging financial technologies.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

282 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Delegates should treat this as an active, moving picture rather than a settled one: FATF's targeted-update reports on virtual-asset compliance are issued on a rolling basis, its list of jurisdictions under increased monitoring changes at every plenary, and the organization has continued to develop guidance on financial inclusion and non-profit-sector protection to guard against the over-broad application of AML/CFT measures. A resolution drafted for this committee should reflect where implementation gaps currently sit, not only the legal architecture that created the obligations in the first place.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

