294 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 135 0 R/T(Money Laundering vs Terrorist Financing)/E(Money Laundering vs Terrorist Financing)/Pg 17 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

295 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 135 0 R/T(Why they overlap)/E(Why they overlap)/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

296 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(A terrorist organization can generate money through criminal activity and then disguise it, or it can raise legitimate-looking donations and use them for prohibited purposes. The same financial infrastructure banks, remitters, cash couriers, companies, charities or digital assets may be abused for both.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

297 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 135 0 R/T(Small-value financing)/E(Small-value financing)/Pg 17 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

298 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(One of the most important misconceptions in terrorist-financing analysis is that every terrorist operation requires large international transfers. Some financing may involve relatively ordinary transactions, personal funds, small businesses, informal transfers, cash or legitimate income diverted to an illicit purpose. This creates a detection challenge because individual transactions may look ordinary when viewed in isolation.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

299 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(The analytical value of an FIU is therefore often found in aggregation and context. A series of small transactions, common counterparties, unusual geographic links, rapid movement of funds or connections to known risk indicators can become meaningful when combined. This is why transaction monitoring should not be reduced to a simple search for unusually large amounts.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

300 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(A major analytical mistake is assuming terrorist financing always involves large transfers. Small payments can matter when aggregated or connected to a network. FIUs therefore need contextual intelligence, not only transaction-size thresholds.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

