351 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 137 0 R/T(Beneficial ownership as a vulnerability)/E(Beneficial ownership as a vulnerability)/Pg 19 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

352 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 137 0 R/E(Beneficial ownership concerns the natural person who ultimately owns or controls a legal entity, even when the formal shareholder or director is another company or nominee. Complex ownership structures can be legitimate, but they can also obscure who controls assets or receives the economic benefit of transactions. For financial intelligence, the challenge is to connect the legal entity appearing in a transaction to the real individuals behind it.This becomes particularly important when criminal networks use legitimate businesses, shell companies or intermediaries to mix illicit proceeds with lawful commercial activity. Effective beneficial-ownership systems therefore require more than collecting company names: authorities need accurate, accessible and verifiable information and mechanisms for sharing it lawfully.Shell companies, nominees and layered ownership structures can obscure who controls an asset or account. Transparent beneficial-ownership systems can help investigators connect financial flows to people, but they must also address data accuracy, access rights and privacy.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

353 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 137 0 R/T(Methods & Case Studies of Terrorist Financing)/E(Methods & Case Studies of Terrorist Financing)/Pg 19 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

