379 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(CASE STUDY 1 - sMALL TRANSACTION, BIG NETWORK)/E(CASE STUDY 1 - sMALL TRANSACTION, BIG NETWORK)/Pg 20 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

380 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(A network can fragment financing across many ordinary transactions to avoid obvious red flags. The lesson is that FIUs should combine transaction data with network analysis, sanctions information, travel or law-enforcement intelligence and contextual indicators rather than relying on a single threshold.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

381 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 138 0 R/T(Organized Crime & Financial Ecosystems)/E(Organized Crime & Financial Ecosystems)/Pg 20 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

382 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(Case Study 2 \204 Abuse of charitable channels)/E(Case Study 2 \204 Abuse of charitable channels)/Pg 20 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

383 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(The misuse of a legitimate non-profit structure illustrates the tension between security and civic space. Blanket restrictions can harm legitimate humanitarian work. A risk-based approach can focus on specific vulnerabilities, transparency, due diligence and proportionate oversight.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

384 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(Organized crime as an economic system)/E(Organized crime as an economic system)/Pg 20 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

385 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(Transnational organized crime is best understood as an ecosystem rather than a single transaction. A network may generate revenue through trafficking, fraud, smuggling, corruption or illegal markets, then place that money into accounts or businesses, move it across borders, convert it into assets and eventually integrate it into apparently legitimate economic activity. Different participants may specialize in different stages.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

386 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(Financial intelligence can expose relationships that conventional criminal investigations miss. A company that appears unrelated to a trafficking network may repeatedly transfer funds to intermediaries; a property purchase may connect several individuals; or a trade transaction may conceal value transfer. \215Following the money\216 therefore does not replace traditional investigation it can reveal the structure that investigators need to understand.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

387 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(Transnational organized crime can involve trafficking, drug markets, migrant smuggling, firearms, cybercrime, environmental crime, counterfeit goods and corruption. Financial flows connect these activities to legitimate economies)/Pg 20 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

388 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(Why criminal networks use legitimate businesses)/E(Why criminal networks use legitimate businesses)/Pg 20 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

389 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 712 0 R/E(To conceal illicit proceeds.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

390 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 713 0 R/E(To move value across borders.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

391 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 714 0 R/E(To create invoices or contracts that look ordinary.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

392 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 715 0 R/E(To gain access to banking and trade infrastructure.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[20]>>
endobj

393 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 716 0 R/E(To corrupt or influence public officials.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[21]>>
endobj

