435 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 140 0 R/T(Technology, Analytics & Digital Assets)/E(Technology, Analytics & Digital Assets)/Pg 22 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

436 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(Financial systems generate enormous amounts of data. Effective FIUs increasingly need automated triage, link analysis, anomaly detection and network visualization. Technology should prioritize analysts rather than replace legal judgment.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

437 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 140 0 R/T(Modern financial intelligence)/E(Modern financial intelligence)/Pg 22 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

438 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 140 0 R/T(Digital assets)/E(Digital assets)/Pg 22 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

439 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(Virtual assets can create new channels for moving value, but the policy challenge is broader than banning technology. Delegates should consider licensing, customer due diligence, suspicious-activity reporting, blockchain analytics, international cooperation and proportionality.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

440 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(FATF extended its AML/CFT standards to virtual assets and virtual asset service providers \(VASPs\) in 2019. Recommendation 16, commonly called the Travel Rule, requires VASPs to collect, verify and transmit identifying information about the originator and beneficiary of a transfer, generally above a threshold of roughly USD/EUR 1,000. This matters directly for FIU work because it determines whether financial intelligence actually reaches a national centre when value moves through a crypto-asset intermediary rather than a bank.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

441 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(Implementation has lagged behind the standard. FATF's own targeted-update reviews of national compliance with Recommendation 15/INR.15 have repeatedly found that a large majority of assessed jurisdictions remain only partially or not compliant, and that gaps are especially pronounced for the Travel Rule itself. Continuing reviews also flag growing exposure through decentralised finance, peer-to-peer transfers, unhosted wallets and stablecoins, none of which route neatly through a single licensed exchange capable of filing a suspicious transaction report.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

442 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(For delegates, the useful drafting question is therefore not whether virtual assets should be regulated in principle, but how a resolution closes specific implementation gaps: requiring licensing and registration of VASPs, supporting interoperable technical standards so Travel Rule data can actually be exchanged across borders, funding blockchain-analytics capacity for lower-resource FIUs, and ensuring virtual-asset activity is not left outside the reporting perimeter that applies to traditional finance.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

443 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 140 0 R/E(Proportionality still matters. Overly broad restrictions on virtual-asset services can push activity toward unregulated or peer-to-peer channels that are harder to monitor than a licensed provider, echoing the de-risking dynamic already discussed for traditional banking. A resolution should pair stronger oversight with realistic implementation timelines for states that are still building basic AML/CFT institutions.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

